# diodic-search.pdf IS tracked -- it is the distribution artifact: a reader
# who opens the repository downloads the paper without a TeX install, and
# GitHub renders it straight from the tree. The Zenodo preprint, DOI
# 10.5281/zenodo.23453407, remains the citable record; the two must be kept in
# step, so rebuild and commit the PDF whenever the .tex changes:
#
#   pdflatex diodic-search.tex   (x3 -- the first pass writes the
#   cross-references, the second reads them, the third settles the page
#   breaks. Without the third pass float placement and the reference
#   format are unstable.)
#
# .gitattributes already carries *.pdf binary, so core.autocrlf cannot corrupt
# the stream. A rebuild is byte-deterministic here; if a diff ever shows up on
# a source-only change, something in the build stopped being reproducible.

# $out/ is the build directory when a pdflatex run is redirected out of the
# tree. Generated, never source -- and a stale build sits untracked in git
# status looking like work to review.
/$out/
/*.tex.orig
/*.tex.bak
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
*.bbl
*.blg
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz
*.nav
*.snm
*.vrb
*.bcf
*.run.xml
*.spl

# /.worktrees/ holds worktrees created by `git worktree add` -- one directory
# per branch, each a full checkout plus its own .git pointer. Scaffolding, never
# source: without this line it shows up as untracked on every git status and can
# be swept into a commit by `git add -A`.
/.worktrees/

/artifacts/
